Thanks — that’s enough to give a clear, practical plan. Below I’ll cover what you can deduct, how to treat the home office (with two calculation options), whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, and the tax forms you’ll need. I’ll also list a few quick follow-ups I need to be sure of.

Quick totals (from what you gave)
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self-employment (graphic design): $12,500
- Interest (Discover): $1,560
- Brokerage interest/dividends (Interactive Brokers): $320
- Total gross income (before deductions): $96,730

What you can deduct / how items are treated
1. Student loan interest
- You paid $4,100 in student loan interest (FedLoan). The student loan interest deduction limits you to a maximum deduction of $2,500 (for 2023). 
- It’s an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (you can claim it whether you itemize or take the standard deduction).
- You should receive Form 1098-E from FedLoan to substantiate this.

2. Charitable contribution ($2,000 to St. Mary’s)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Cash gifts of $250+ require a written acknowledgement (you said you have a receipt — keep it).
- If you take the standard deduction ($27,700 for Married Filing Jointly in 2023), you cannot separately deduct this gift.

3. Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are expenses tied to your self-employment (graphic design) they are deductible on Schedule C as business expenses.
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by tax law). Please confirm which they are.

4. Home office (130 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Because you have self-employment income from graphic design, you can claim a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business and it is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.). W‑2 employees cannot claim this.
- Two methods to compute the deduction:

  A. Simplified method (easy)
  - $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
  - 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 deduction (reported on Schedule C).

  B. Actual-expense method (may give a larger deduction but requires more work)
  - Business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% (≈16.67%).
  - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Rent portion allocable to office = 20,640 × 16.6667% ≈ $3,440.
  - You can also allocate a share of utilities and other allowable home expenses to the business portion (you didn’t list utilities; include them if you have them).
  - Actual method requires Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and you transfer the result to Schedule C.

- Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business; any excess may be carried forward. Also deducting home office on your Schedule C reduces net self-employment income and therefore reduces self-employment tax.

5. Self-employment tax and related deduction
- Because self-employment net income (after Schedule C expenses like home office and business expenses) is over $400, you’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare).
- SE tax is calculated on Schedule SE. Half of your SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), which lowers your AGI.

6. Interest & brokerage amounts
- Report interest from Discover (likely 1099-INT) and Interactive Brokers (1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B as appropriate). Brokerage income may also include dividends or capital gains—if you sold securities you may need Form 8949 / Schedule D.

Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2023): $27,700.
- Your only itemizable item you listed is the $2,000 charitable gift (plus any state/local taxes you paid — you didn’t provide SALT info). With just $2,000 in charitable donations (and absent significant mortgage interest, property tax, or large medical expenses), the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
