Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through how to treat each item and what to expect for 2023. I’m not a CPA, so consider this general guidance and confirm with tax software or a tax professional for filing.

Summary judgement (short)
- You will almost certainly be better off taking the 2023 standard deduction ($27,700 MFJ) rather than itemizing based on the items you listed.
- You should report your graphic design activity on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home‑office deduction is available for that business; using the actual method (Form 8829) looks far more favorable than the simplified method for your numbers.
- Student loan interest: you can only deduct up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line) and that is subject to MAGI phaseouts; with the income you gave you should qualify for the full $2,500.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $780) are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law unless they are business expenses for your self‑employment activity (then put them on Schedule C).

Detailed breakdown and calculations

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $12,500 — report on Schedule C. If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those amounts.
- Interest: $1,560 (Discover) + $320 (Interactive Brokers) = $1,880 — report as taxable interest. Because total interest > $1,500, you’ll also complete Schedule B.
- Have and keep Forms: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, 1099‑INTs, and Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.

2) Home office deduction (you said 130 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
  - Actual method: allocate business share = 130 / 780 = 1/6 (≈16.6667%). Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Home office share of rent = $20,640 × 1/6 = $3,440.
- Because you meet the “exclusive and principal place of business” requirement for a home office for your self‑employment, you can take the home office deduction on Schedule C. The actual method (using Form 8829) gives a much larger deduction here ($3,440 vs $650).
- Note: actual method may require additional allocation of utilities, insurance, etc., if you have them. Use Form 8829 for the actual method. Simplified method does not use Form 8829.

3) Self‑employment tax and adjustment
- Using actual home office example (net business = $12,500 − $3,440 = $9,060):
  - Net SE earnings for tax = $9,060 × 0.9235 = $8,372.91
  - Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) ≈ $8,372.91 × 15.3% ≈ $1,280.36 (reported on Schedule SE)
  - Deductible portion (above‑the‑line on Schedule 1) = half of SE tax ≈ $640.18
- Using simplified home office ($650) gives a higher SE tax and slightly larger half‑SE deduction; overall the actual method gives a bigger net tax benefit because the home office expense is larger.

4) Student loan interest
- You paid $4,100 in student loan interest, but the allowed deduction is up to $2,500/year. You can deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseout.
- For 2023 the phaseout for married filing jointly is roughly $145,000–$175,000 MAGI. Based on the wages and business amounts you gave, your MAGI will be well under $145,000, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction.
- Keep Form 1098‑E (or lender statement) showing interest paid.

5) Charitable contribution
- Cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank: $2,000 (you have a receipt). This is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). It does not reduce AGI directly.
- Since the standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 is $27,700, and your listed itemizable amounts ($2,000 charity + likely SALT up to $10,000 if you have state & property taxes) appear far below $27,700, you will very likely take the standard deduction instead of itemizing. Example: 2k charity + 10k SALT = $12k < $27,700.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses (i.e., you were an employee and incurred them), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended).
- If they are ordinary and necessary expenses for your self‑employed graphic design work (e.g., supplies, software, client travel), report them on Schedule C instead.

7) Recommended approach for you
- Report W‑2 on Form 1040.
- Report self‑employment on Schedule C. Claim the home office deduction via the actual method (Form 8829) — it yields ~$3,440 vs $650, which looks better for your numbers.
- File Schedule SE to calculate self‑employment tax; claim half of SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Claim student loan interest (up to $2,500) as an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (you should qualify).
- Use the standard deduction ($27,700) unless you have additional itemizable deductions you didn’t list that push total itemized above $27,700.
- If total taxable interest > $1,500, complete Schedule B.

8) Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: half SE tax, student loan interest)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business)
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual home office method
  - Schedule B (if interest + dividends > $1,500)
  - Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (likely not)
- State:
  - Illinois Form IL‑1040 (state return)

9) Records to keep
- Receipts and documentation for charitable donation ($2,000 receipt).
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), 1099‑INT, W‑2, any 1099‑NEC.
- Proof of rent payments, a floor plan or calculation showing square footage of home and office space, and any utility bills if using actual method.
- Records of any business expenses you claim on Schedule C.

10) Other notes
- Because you have self‑employment income and SE tax, you may have an estimated tax/quarterly payment obligation for 2024 if you expect similar income next year.
- If you expect larger business expenses, or have mortgage interest or high property taxes you didn’t list, recheck whether itemizing could become favorable.
- Consider using tax software (which will walk through Forms C, SE, 8829) or consult a CPA for a final filing, especially since you have a mixture of W‑2 and self‑employment income.

If you want, I can:
- Run a ballpark tax calculation for 2023 showing estimated taxable income and federal tax due/refund under both the actual and simplified home office methods, or
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing, or
- Draft an email/summary you can send to your tax preparer.

Which of those would you like next?

