Thanks — I can walk through this and give a clear recommendation plus the forms you'll need. A couple quick clarifying questions before I finalize anything:
- Is the $4,100 of student-loan interest the amount you paid in 2023? (Only interest paid during the tax year 2023 is deductible; earlier years don’t count.)
- Do you have 1098‑E from FedLoan for 2023 (or other proof of interest paid in 2023)?
- Do you have any other deductible expenses (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax, large medical expenses, IRA contributions, or other Schedule C business expenses besides the home office and $780 unreimbursed employee expenses)?

Below is a summary based on what you gave and general IRS rules (not a substitute for personalized advice from a CPA).

Quick conclusions
- You will report the $12,500 graphic-design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). The W‑2 and interest go on Form 1040.
- The $1,560 (Discover) and $320 (Interactive Brokers) are taxable interest and reported on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 / interest lines), assuming you received 1099‑INT/1099‑B reporting those amounts.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA). The $780 unreimbursed expenses are not deductible.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (Form 1040 adjustment) up to $2,500 for 2023, subject to income phaseouts — but only interest paid in 2023 and only if your MAGI is below the phaseout (for MFJ 2023: starts at $145,000, phases out by $175,000). You must have Form 1098‑E or other records.
- Home-office deduction: available for your self‑employment income. You can use the simplified method (130 sq ft × $5 = $650) or the actual method (allocating a portion of rent and eligible home costs). Based on rent only, the actual method looks better here (see numbers below).
- Standard vs itemize: For 2023 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $27,700. Your listed itemizable items (charity $2,000) are far below that, so you should take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical bills, etc.). Student loan interest is an adjustment (above‑the‑line), not an itemized deduction.

Detailed treatment and forms you’ll need
1) Income reporting
- W‑2 income (Midwest Health Systems): Form 1040, wages line.
- Self‑employment income ($12,500): Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest income ($1,560 + $320): reported on 1040 (Schedule 1 if required) — keep 1099‑INT / 1099‑B info.

2) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- You must file Schedule SE to calculate self‑employment tax (~15.3% applied to 92.35% of net self‑employment income).
- You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).

3) Home office
- Because you’re self‑employed, you can claim business use of home for the 130‑sq‑ft exclusive office.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 130 = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Actual: allocate a percentage of rent and other qualifying expenses. Your office is 130/780 = 16.666...% (1/6).
    - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
    - Rent portion allocable to office = 1/6 × $20,640 = $3,440.
    - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., you’d allocate the business portion of those too — increasing the deduction.
  - To use actual method you complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C. The deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business for the year; any excess is carried forward in many cases.
- Recommendation: with only rent shown, actual method (≈ $3,440) looks better than simplified ($650).

4) Charitable donation
- $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank: deductible only if you itemize. Because the standard deduction for MFJ is $27,700, you will likely take the standard deduction unless you have more itemizable deductions (state/local taxes up to $10,000 cap, mortgage interest, large medical, etc.). Keep the receipt.

5) Student loan interest
- If you paid student loan interest in 2023 you can deduct up to $2,500 on Schedule 1 (adjustment). You’ll need Form 1098‑E or other proof. Phaseouts apply for higher AGI for MFJ ($145k–$175k for 2023).

6) State return
- You’ll also file Illinois Form IL‑1040 for state income tax.

Forms checklist
- Federal: Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — interest income reporting and student loan interest adjustment
  - Schedule C (self‑employment income and expenses)
  - Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)
  - Form 8829 (if you choose actual home‑office method)
- State: Illinois Form IL‑1040
- Keep: W‑2, 1099‑INT(s), 1098‑E (student loan interest), receipts for charity, records for home‑office allocation (floorplan, rent, utility bills), client invoices, and any 1099‑NEC if issued.

Rough numeric example (to show effect of home‑office choice)
Assumptions: only business expense is home office; student loan interest deductible up to $2,500 (you paid enough in 2023); interest totals are taxable as you reported.
- Annual rent = $20,640; home office = 1/6 of home = 16.666...%
A) Actual home‑office method
  - Home‑office deduction: $3,440
  - Net self‑employment income ≈ $12,500 − $3,440 = $9,060
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ $1,281 (Schedule SE)
  - Half SE tax deduction ≈ $640 (adjustment)
  - AGI roughly: 82,350 (W‑2) + 1,880 (interest) + 9,060 (net SE) − 640 (half SE) − 2,500 (student loan interest) ≈ $90,150
  - Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $90,150 − $27,700 = $62,450

B) Simplified method
  - Home‑office deduction: $650
  - Net SE income ≈ $11,850
  - SE tax ≈ $1,675; half SE deduction ≈ $837
  - AGI roughly ≈ $92,743
  - Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $65,043

So actual method appears better here (saves a few thousand in taxable income), but the precise numbers depend on any other business expenses you have and exact SE tax calculation.

Other important notes / next steps
- Confirm the 2023 amount of student‑loan interest and get 1098‑E.
- Collect all 1099‑INT / 1099‑B, any 1099‑NEC (if clients issued them), W‑2.
- If you expect to owe $1,000+ tax when filing, and you didn’t have enough withholding, you should consider estimated quarterly payments for 2024 to avoid penalties.
- Keep documentation for the home office (floor plan showing exclusive use, lease statements, rent paid, utility bills, receipts).
- Consider whether any additional business expenses exist (software subscriptions, supplies, mileage, phone/internet percentage) — those reduce SE tax and income tax.
- I’m not a CPA — for complex questions (carryforwards, large deductions, retirement plan contributions, minimizing state tax) it’s a good idea to consult a tax professional.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a checklist email to an accountant with these numbers and attach the receipts (if you want me to forward);
- Prepare a step‑by‑step worksheet summarizing the exact numbers if you confirm the student‑loan interest paid in 2023 and any additional business expenses you may have. Which would you prefer?

