Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is enough to give actionable guidance. I’ll summarize what you can (likely) deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and the forms you’ll need. I’ll also call out a few clarifying questions where I need more info to be precise.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Most likely you’ll take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have sizable mortgage interest, property tax, or other itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.
- Report the graphic-design income on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). The home-office deduction (Schedule C + Form 8829 or the simplified method) will reduce your self-employment taxable income.
- Student-loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (Schedule 1) but is limited to $2,500 for 2023 (and phases out at higher MAGI). You can’t deduct the full $4,100 unless the $4,100 is interest paid in 2023 and you’re under the income phaseout — see clarifying questions below.
- Interest from bank and brokerage is taxable and likely requires Schedule B because your total interest ($1,560 + $320 = $1,880) exceeds $1,500.

What you can deduct (and how)
1) W-2 wages from Midwest Health Systems ($82,350)
- Report on Form 1040 via the W-2. Unreimbursed employee expenses (if the $780 is from the W-2 job) are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension) unless you have a specific exception (military, certain performing artists, etc.). So if the $780 is unreimbursed employee expenses from the W-2 job, it’s not deductible.

2) Self-employment income (graphic design): $12,500
- Report gross receipts on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Subtract ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., the $780 if those are business expenses — supplies, software, mileage, etc.). If the $780 relates to your self-employment, include it on Schedule C.
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is computed on Schedule SE. You pay SE tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net SE income). You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- If clients issued 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, include them and reconcile totals with your records.

3) Home office (130 sq ft exclusive use in 780 sq ft home; rent $1,720/month)
- You have a deductible home-office expense because you’re self-employed and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  b) Regular method: compute proportionate share of actual home expenses. Business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% (≈16.67%).
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Rent portion deductible = $20,640 × 16.6667% ≈ $3,440.
     - You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Direct expenses (repairs specific to the office) are fully deductible; indirect expenses (rent, utilities) are deductible by the business percentage.
     - Form 8829 is used with Schedule C if you use the regular method.
  - Choose the method that gives the larger deduction (subject to rules). Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed gross income from that business. Any excess may be carried forward (regular method) or disallowed.

4) Student loan interest — $4,100 (you listed)
- Student loan interest deduction is an “above-the-line” adjustment on Schedule 1 (up to $2,500 for 2023), subject to MAGI phase-outs.
- For Married Filing Jointly in 2023 the phase-out range begins at MAGI $145,000 and ends at $175,000. If your joint MAGI is below $145,000 you can take the full $2,500; between $145k–$175k the deduction is reduced; above $175k none is allowed.
- Important: this deduction only applies to interest paid in the tax year (interest paid in 2023). Also you should receive Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer reporting interest paid in 2023.

5) Charitable donation — $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank
- Charitable contributions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Given the standard deduction for MFJ ($27,700), a $2,000 cash gift by itself will not make itemizing beneficial. (There is no general above-the-line deduction for charitable gifts for 2023.)
- Keep your receipt for substantiation.

6) Interest income: Discover ($1,560) and Interactive Brokers ($320)
- Report on Schedule B if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500. You have $1,880 total interest, so include Schedule B.

Other notes
- Half of your self-employment tax is deductible (Schedule 1) as an adjustment to income.
- If you use the regular home-office method and you rent the unit, you won't claim depreciation on a property you rent (depreciation applies to owners). Depreciation only applies if you own the property.
- If you have state income tax or property tax payments, or mortgage interest, those matter for the itemize vs standard decision (SALT cap $10,000).
- Keep supporting documentation: W-2, 1099-INT, 1099-NEC, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), receipts for charitable gifts, rent statements and photos/layout showing office dimensions, receipts for business expenses.

Which is better — itemize or standard?
- With the items you listed, standard deduction ($27,700 MFJ) is very likely better than itemizing ($2,000 charitable + other likely small items).
- Student loan interest is not an itemized deduction anyway — it reduces your AGI up to $2,500 (if eligible).
- If you later find substantial deductible items you didn’t list (large mortgage interest, high property taxes, large state taxes up to $10k, large medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, or larger charitable contributions), re-check itemizing.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest adjustment, and to report Schedule C net business income on line(s) that flow to 1040
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report graphic-design income/expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular home-office method
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — since total interest > $1,500
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (likely not required)
- Keep/forms to expect: 1099-NEC (from clients if issued), 1099-INT (from Discover and Interactive Brokers), 1098‑E (student loan interest)

Estimated tax / withholding
- Because of self-employment income, you may owe self-employment tax and income tax on that income. If clients did not withhold, consider making (or having made) estimated tax payments for 2023 (Form 1040-ES) and adjusting withholding for 2024 if needed.

A few clarifying questions so I can be precise / run numbers
1. Is the $82,350 W-2 amount your combined (you + spouse) wages, or only one spouse’s wages? Do you (or your spouse) have other wage income?
2. Is the $4,100 student-loan interest amount interest you paid in 2023 (and do you have Form 1098‑E for 2023)? Or is $4,100 cumulative interest over prior years?
3. Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the graphic design work (i.e., purchases for your freelance business) or employee expenses related to the W-2 job?
4. Do you rent the home (i.e., you are not the homeowner)? (You wrote you pay $1,720/month — I’m assuming that’s rent.)
5. Do you have other potential itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, large state taxes, other charitable gifts) not listed?

If you want, I can:
