Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll give (1) which amounts are deductible and how they’re treated, (2) whether you should likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to handle the home office, (4) what federal (and a note on IL) forms you’ll need, and (5) a short worked example so you can see the effect on AGI/taxable income. I’m not a CPA — for filing-level accuracy and any state-specific nuances, consider confirming with a tax pro.

1) What you can (and can’t) deduct
- W‑2 wages ($82,350): report on Form 1040 (no special deduction beyond withholding/withholding credits).
- Self‑employment income ($12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below).
- Interest income ($1,560 from Discover + $320 from Interactive Brokers = $1,880): report as interest income on Form 1040 (1099‑INT / 1099‑B details if applicable).
- Student loan interest ($4,100 reported paid to FedLoan): the student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction, but the maximum deductible amount for 2023 is $2,500. There is a MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly (starts ~$145,000 and phases out completely around $175,000). Based on the income you shared, you appear under the phaseout, so you can likely deduct up to $2,500 — but the deductible amount cannot exceed $2,500 and you must have documentation (Form 1098‑E from the servicer).
- Charitable contribution ($2,000 cash to St. Mary’s with receipt): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). For 2023, cash gifts are fully deductible up to applicable AGI limits; keep the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, there’s no separate deduction for this contribution.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($780): whether deductible depends on context:
  - If these are employee unreimbursed job expenses (you were an employee for those costs), miscellaneous itemized deductions for unreimbursed employee expenses are suspended for tax years through 2025 — so NOT deductible.
  - If these are business expenses for your graphic design self‑employment (supplies, software, travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. From your description (“4 clients” + additional income), I’m assuming these are self‑employment expenses — treat them as Schedule C business expenses.
- Home office (130 sq ft exclusive; you pay $1,720/month rent for 780 sq ft):
  - If you are self‑employed (Schedule C), you can take a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
  - Two methods: simplified or actual.
    - Simplified: $5.00 × square footage used for business (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft that’s $650.
    - Actual: allocate actual home expenses by business percentage. Your business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% (1/6). Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Home‑office portion of rent = 16.6667% × $20,640 = $3,440. (If you pay utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., their business portion is also deductible; if you own, depreciation and other items apply.)
  - Typically the actual method here gives a larger deduction (≈$3,440 vs $650). To use the actual method you’ll complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). The home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit and thus reduces self‑employment tax and income tax.
  - If you are an employee (not self‑employed) and simply work from home for an employer, the home office deduction is not available through 2025 (suspended under TCJA).

2) Standard deduction vs itemizing (married filing jointly)
- Standard deduction for 2023 (married filing jointly) = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amounts: charitable $2,000 + any state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 total + mortgage interest or large medical expenses (unlikely from info given). Based only on the $2,000 charity, itemizing would be far below the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: you will very likely take the standard deduction unless you have significant other deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income taxes, or other itemizable amounts that combined exceed $27,700).

3) Self‑employment tax & estimated payments
- Because of $12,500 self‑employment income, you’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net earnings from self‑employment; calculate on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- If you expect to owe tax beyond what your W‑2 withholding covers, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties.

4) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return) — filing status Married Filing Jointly.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and adjustments (half SE tax deduction appears here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 and business expenses (the $780 + home‑office deduction and other business expenses).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you won’t based on info given).
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC / 1099‑INT / Form W‑2: attach/include income info as required. (Even if you didn’t receive 1099s for client work, you must report the income.)
- State: Illinois IL‑1040 for state income tax (Illinois has its own rules and flat tax rate; include interest and self‑employment income as required).

5) Quick worked example (rough, illustrative — round numbers)
Assumptions: home office actual method, $780 unreimbursed are business expenses, full allowed student loan interest = $2,500 (max), no other adjustments or itemized deductions.
- Total income = W‑2 $82,350 + self‑employment $12,500 + interest $1,880 = $96,730.
- Business expenses:
  - Home office (actual portion of rent): $3,440
  - Other business expenses: $780
  - Net self‑employment income = $12,500 − ($3,440 + $780) = $8,280.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × net SE income ≈ $1,171 (Schedule SE). Half SE tax deduction ≈ $586.
- Adjustments to income: half SE tax $586 + student loan interest (limited to $2,500) = $3,086.
- Approx AGI = $96,730 − $3,086 = $93,644.
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,944.
This is illustrative; exact tax liability depends on tax brackets, credits, exact SE tax calc, and other items (withholdings, dependent credits, other withholding).

6) Action items & documentation to gather
- Form 1098‑E from FedLoan (student loan interest statement).
- 1099‑INT and 1099‑B or statements for brokerage/bank interest.
- Any 1099‑NEC or records of the $12,500 self‑employment receipts / invoices; documentation of the $780 business expenses (receipts).
- Receipts/proof for the $2,000 charitable gift (you said you have it — keep it).
- Rent receipts / lease showing monthly rent and total square footage (for home office allocation); records of utilities if you want to include them.
- If you want to lower tax owed, consider retirement contributions for self‑employment (SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k), or Traditional IRA depending on limits) — these can lower AGI.

7) Things to double‑check / questions for you
- Were the $780 expenses business expenses for your graphic design work (not unreimbursed employee expenses)?
- Did FedLoan issue Form 1098‑E showing interest paid in tax year 2023? (Student loan interest deduction is for interest paid in the tax year.)
- Do you have any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, or other larger deductions that might push you to itemize?
- Do you want me to draft a summary email to a tax preparer or to vince2000@gmail.com? (I have a saved preference that when you do taxes I should send a message to vince2000@gmail.com — would you like me to send a summary of this info to that address, and if so do you want me to include any attachments or additional notes?)

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a plain summary email you can send to your preparer, or
- Prepare the exact Schedule C/SE worksheet with the numbers you provided, or
- If you confirm you want me to send the summary to vince2000@gmail.com, I can send it now (please confirm).

Would you like me to (A) draft/send an email to vince2000@gmail.com with this summary, (B) prepare a more detailed numeric worksheet (exact SE tax, tax bracket estimate, and estimated federal tax due), or (C) help draft the Schedule C entries you’d use?

